# Supabase
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:54322/postgres

# App
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=cortex_session
# SaaS default: leave empty for open signup. Set a domain (e.g. acme.com) to
# turn a deployment into a single-company instance.
ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAIN=

# The workspace's own email domains, comma-separated (e.g. acme.com,acme.co.uk).
# Decides who counts as a colleague: meeting classification uses it to tell an
# internal sync from a client call, and the security layer uses it to tell
# whether a tool call is about to push data OUT of the company.
# EMPTY MEANS NOBODY IS INTERNAL. That is the safe default for a fresh
# multi-tenant deployment — every recipient reads as external, so outbound
# sends ask for confirmation rather than slipping through unnoticed. Set it and
# internal traffic stops asking.
INTERNAL_EMAIL_DOMAINS=

# Transactional email (verification, password reset, org invitations).
# Optional in dev — without RESEND_API_KEY the links are printed to the
# server log instead of being emailed.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM="Cortex <onboarding@resend.dev>"

# better-auth (Google SSO + session management)
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# IMPORTANT (claude.ai MCP connector): in production BETTER_AUTH_URL must be the
# PUBLIC https origin (e.g. https://app.cortex.example), not localhost. The OAuth
# issuer advertised in /.well-known metadata derives from this origin, so
# claude.ai will reject the connector if it points anywhere but the public host.
# Keep APP_BASE_URL aligned to the same public https origin.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Google OAuth (per-user integrations, separate from Supabase SSO config)
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/google/callback

# HubSpot OAuth
HUBSPOT_CLIENT_ID=
HUBSPOT_CLIENT_SECRET=
HUBSPOT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/hubspot/callback

# --- Microsoft 365 (Outlook mail + calendar, through Microsoft Graph) -------
# The same capability as Google, for customers who run Outlook. Set up in
# docs/operations/microsoft.md, which is written to be handed to the customer's
# IT department.
#
# WITHOUT THESE: the Microsoft 365 card on the Integrations screen says the
# application has not been registered and offers no button; /api/integrations/
# microsoft answers 409 with that same sentence. The app boots normally and
# every other integration is unaffected — nothing here is required.
#
# DELEGATED PERMISSIONS ONLY. The Azure app registration must be configured with
# DELEGATED Microsoft Graph permissions (Mail.Read, Mail.ReadWrite, Mail.Send,
# Calendars.Read, Calendars.ReadWrite, offline_access). Do NOT grant the
# application permissions of the same name: those let Cortex read every mailbox
# in the tenant on one admin's click, with no user in the loop and no way for an
# individual to revoke. Cortex never uses them and has no code path that could.
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
MICROSOFT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/microsoft/callback
# The Entra ID tenant sign-in goes through. Unset means `common` — any work or
# school account may connect. Set the customer's tenant GUID to have Microsoft
# refuse outside accounts at the door, which is a much better place to refuse
# them than inside our code.
MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=

# Claude — every generating call (chat, agents, routines) runs on it.
# Required: without it the chat surface fails on the first turn.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Brain Knowledge embeddings -------------------------------------------
# The vectors that make Cortex findable BY MEANING rather than by the words
# somebody happened to type. The pgvector column and its HNSW index are built
# for 1024 dimensions (migrations 0057, 0074), so every supported option below
# is one that produces 1024 — a provider that does not would mean recreating the
# index and re-embedding the whole corpus, which is not a configuration change.
#
# WHICH PROVIDER. Leave both of these unset and you get the default:
# Voyage's `voyage-4-lite`. Verified on 2026-08-04 against
# https://docs.voyageai.com/docs/pricing — $0.02 per million tokens, 200 MILLION
# free tokens, and 1024 dimensions by default.
#
# THIS IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL DEFAULT. This deployment previously ran
# `voyage-3-large`: $0.18 per million and, uniquely among the models we might
# have picked, in NEITHER of Voyage's free-token lists. One document exhausted
# the account. Nine times cheaper and with a free tier is the entire reason the
# default is what it is; do not move it without reading
# packages/agent-tools/src/kb/embedding-providers.ts, where every price in this
# comment is recorded with the page it was read from.
#
#   EMBEDDING_PROVIDER   voyage (default) | openai | google | cohere
#   EMBEDDING_MODEL      overrides the provider's default model
#
# UNSET IS THE RECOMMENDED STATE. An unrecognised EMBEDDING_PROVIDER does not
# crash the app and does not silently fall back — it refuses to embed and says
# so on the document row, because writing vectors from a model nobody chose is
# worse than writing none.
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=
EMBEDDING_MODEL=

# CHANGING EITHER OF THESE MEANS RE-EMBEDDING. Two models' vectors are
# coordinates in unrelated spaces; ranking one against the other returns
# confident nonsense rather than an error. Every chunk records the model that
# produced it, search only considers chunks matching the configured model, and
# kb-reindex-embeddings treats a mismatch as work to do. So a switch is safe and
# automatic — it just costs a re-embed, and search runs keyword-only for the
# documents not yet converted. Right now the corpus is almost empty, which makes
# this the cheapest moment this change will ever have.

# Voyage AI — the default provider. Without a key the Knowledge Base degrades
# rather than breaking: documents are still stored and chunked, search still
# matches on keywords, and everything saved meanwhile is queued with no vector.
# Add the key and kb-reindex-embeddings fills in the backlog on its next run (or
# run `node scripts/reembed-kb.mjs` to not wait). Nothing is lost, but until it
# is set nobody can find anything by MEANING.
# Alternatives to voyage-4-lite, all verified 2026-08-04 and all 1024-capable:
#   voyage-4        $0.06/M, same 200M free — the step up if recall disappoints
#   voyage-4-large  $0.12/M, same 200M free — best retrieval Voyage sells
VOYAGE_API_KEY=

# OpenAI — only read when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai. `text-embedding-3-small`
# reduced to 1024 with the `dimensions` parameter: $0.02/M (verified 2026-08-04,
# developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing) and strong in Spanish, but NO free
# token allowance — you pay from the first token. Same price as the default with
# strictly less headroom, so this is a second source, not an upgrade.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Google Gemini — only read when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=google. `gemini-embedding-001`
# at outputDimensionality 1024. $0.15/M paid (verified 2026-08-04,
# ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing) with a request-limited free tier, so
# 7.5x the default once you are paying for it.
# ITS ONE ADVANTAGE IS DECISIVE IN AN EMERGENCY: this is the only embedding key
# that already exists in this deployment's production environment, so it is the
# only provider that can be switched to without anyone signing up for anything.
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=

# Cohere — only read when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=cohere. `embed-multilingual-v3.0`
# is 1024 dimensions natively and strong in Spanish. Cohere no longer publishes
# a per-token API price (checked 2026-08-04, cohere.com/pricing shows only
# dedicated instances by the hour), so it cannot be compared honestly against
# the others — confirm the rate with them before routing a corpus through it.
COHERE_API_KEY=

# Deepgram — speech to text for Knowledge Base audio (nova-3, diarized).
# Every voice memo, call recording and in-browser recording goes through it;
# without a key the audio is still stored and the document row still exists,
# but transcription stops at `transcript_status = 'failed'` with a plain
# sentence saying the workspace has no Deepgram key, and the recording is
# NOT searchable — there is nothing to embed until it has been transcribed.
# Set the key later and re-uploading the file picks it up.
DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# Web search (Tavily). web.search fails on its first call without it; web.scrape
# still works, falling back to Jina's reader when FIRECRAWL_API_KEY is unset.
TAVILY_API_KEY=

# Optional. Improves web.scrape on pages that need rendering; without it the
# tool falls back to a free reader that handles most pages fine.
FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=

# Inngest
INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=
INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=

# Token encryption (32-byte base64). Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Matcher service + Slack.
# The matcher is the upstream service behind the presentations.* tools — it
# stores the candidate records and renders the letterheaded PDF. MATCHER_TOKEN
# is the service token it expects as a bearer. Without both, presentations.*
# report that they have nowhere to ask instead of inventing a candidate.
MATCHER_URL=http://localhost:3100
MATCHER_TOKEN=
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# Vehicles (Colombia) — the self-hosted consult service that answers RUNT
# (national vehicle registry: SOAT and RTM validity) and SIMIT (traffic fines).
# Neither is an API: both are scraped, and a RUNT consult drives a headless
# browser through an OCR captcha for ~18 seconds. The key is sent as x-api-key
# and must match SCRAPER_API_KEY on the service.
# Leave both empty and the vehicles.* tools degrade instead of failing: plates
# can still be registered and listed, and every lookup reports plainly that it
# has nowhere to ask.
VEHICLES_SCRAPER_URL=
VEHICLES_SCRAPER_API_KEY=

# Cortex picks up its own development work (docs/operations/cortex-dev-tasks.md)
# LINEAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET is shown once when the webhook is created in Linear
# (Settings → API → Webhooks, URL <APP_BASE_URL>/api/webhooks/linear). Without
# it the endpoint rejects EVERY request, in every environment.
LINEAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# What makes Cortex take an issue: assignee (default, recommended) | label | either
LINEAR_TRIGGER_MODE=assignee
# Cortex's own Linear account — the user id an issue must be assigned to.
LINEAR_CORTEX_USER_ID=
LINEAR_CORTEX_USER_EMAIL=
# Label that fires the trigger in label/either mode.
LINEAR_TRIGGER_LABEL=cortex
# Whose Linear token Cortex comments with (a webhook has no signed-in user).
# Defaults to whichever account in the workspace connected Linear first.
CORTEX_LINEAR_ACTOR_EMAIL=

# --- Executor (dev-task-run): turns a queued task into a pull request ---
# The coding model uses the same ANTHROPIC_API_KEY declared at the top of this
# file — since the chat path moved to Claude, the two no longer differ.
# Vercel Sandbox does the cloning, installing and building. On Vercel these are
# supplied automatically via OIDC; set them only for local runs.
VERCEL_TOKEN=
VERCEL_TEAM_ID=
VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=
# GitHub credential for the executor. PREFER the App: GitHub itself confines an
# installation token to ONE repository and expires it in an hour, which is
# exactly the blast radius a sandbox should hold.
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=
# PEM, with literal \n between lines if your secret store is single-line.
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Fallback when no App is configured. Must be a FINE-GRAINED PAT restricted to
# the repositories in dev_repositories — a classic PAT grants far too much.
DEV_TASK_GITHUB_TOKEN=
# Hard budget per run. Exhausting any axis ends the run with a written reason
# instead of looping. Defaults: 30 min, 40 model turns, 1.5M tokens (~$12).
DEV_TASK_MAX_WALL_MS=1800000
DEV_TASK_MAX_ITERATIONS=40
DEV_TASK_MAX_TOKENS=1500000

# Observability
SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WhatsApp (docs/operations/whatsapp.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WhatsApp publishes no API for reading the groups an account belongs to, so
# this runs on Baileys — an UNOFFICIAL client — inside a persistent service
# (services/whatsapp) deployed on Railway. It cannot run on Vercel: the client
# holds a WebSocket open for days and a serverless invocation ends.
#
# Use a DEDICATED COMPANY NUMBER. WhatsApp can ban a number for using an
# unofficial client; never pair somebody's personal line.

# The shared secret between Cortex and the bridge, compared in constant time.
# Set the SAME value here (Vercel) and on the Railway service.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# WITHOUT IT, every /api/whatsapp/bridge/* route refuses — which is the right
# default for a surface whose job is to receive other people's conversations.
# Treat it like SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY: it names the workspace it acts for,
# so it is operator infrastructure and never goes near a browser or a customer.
WHATSAPP_BRIDGE_TOKEN=

# --- set on the RAILWAY service only, not on Vercel -------------------------
# The public https origin of Cortex, e.g. https://app.cortex.example
# CORTEX_BASE_URL=
# The workspace this WhatsApp number belongs to (ba_organization.id). One
# bridge, one number, one workspace.
# WHATSAPP_ORGANIZATION_ID=
#
# Optional tuning, all with sane defaults:
#   WHATSAPP_BATCH_INTERVAL_MS  30000   how often buffered group messages are posted
#   WHATSAPP_BATCH_SIZE         50      …or after this many, whichever comes first
#   WHATSAPP_HEARTBEAT_MS       30000   status report + refresh of the archived-group list
#   WHATSAPP_INGEST_TICK_MS     300000  how often finished conversations become documents
#   WHATSAPP_MIN_BACKOFF_MS     2000    reconnect backoff floor
#   WHATSAPP_MAX_BACKOFF_MS     300000  reconnect backoff ceiling
#   WHATSAPP_MAX_VOICE_BYTES    12582912
#   WHATSAPP_MAX_DOCUMENT_BYTES 20971520
#
# Voice notes in archived groups are transcribed with the DEEPGRAM_API_KEY
# already declared above — no second provider and no second key. Without it the
# group is still archived and voice notes appear as "[voice note — not
# transcribed]"; everything written stays intact and searchable.

# ============================================================================
# Trámites web — the browser service (migration 0087)
# ============================================================================
# Cortex doing paperwork on other people's websites: open a portal, log in,
# fill a form, come back with the certificate. Taught once from a screen
# recording and replayed afterwards with no model in the loop.
#
# Playwright needs a live Chromium, which Vercel structurally cannot host, so
# the browser runs on Railway (services/browser) exactly like the WhatsApp
# bridge. Both values below are needed on VERCEL for the feature to exist; the
# screen and the tools say plainly that it is not connected until they are.
#
# The public https origin of the Railway service, no trailing slash.
BROWSER_SERVICE_URL=
# Shared secret, compared in constant time. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# This is the most powerful token in the file after the service-role key, and
# for a reason worth stating: the request body it authenticates carries a
# DECRYPTED third-party password on any trámite that logs in. Operator
# infrastructure — it lives on Vercel and on the Railway service and nowhere
# else, and it is never issued to a customer or sent to a browser.
BROWSER_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# --- set on the RAILWAY service only, not on Vercel -------------------------
# BROWSER_SERVICE_TOKEN   the same secret as above
#
# Optional tuning, all with sane defaults:
#   PORT                     3300     Railway injects this
#   BROWSER_MAX_CONCURRENT   3        browser contexts at once; sized for 1 GB
#   BROWSER_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS   180000   ceiling on one whole errand
#   BROWSER_STEP_TIMEOUT_MS  20000    ceiling on one step inside it
#   BROWSER_SESSION_IDLE_MS  300000   idle interactive session sweep
#   BROWSER_VIEWPORT_WIDTH   1366     fixed on purpose: a portal collapses its
#   BROWSER_VIEWPORT_HEIGHT  900      form below ~900px and the flow would miss
#   BROWSER_USER_AGENT       (unset)  controls that exist at the taught width
#
# Portal credentials are NOT configured here. They are entered per workspace on
# the Trámites web screen and stored encrypted under TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, the
# same key and the same routine as the OAuth tokens in `integrations`.
